    \field{labelyear}{2024}
    \field{labeldatesource}{}
    \field{sortinit}{R}
    \field{sortinithash}{R}
    \field{abstract}{%
    Over the last decades, a multitude of results in educational and
  psychological research have shown that the implementation of multiple
  external representations (MERs) in educational contexts represents a valuable
  tool for fostering learning and problem-solving skills. The context of
  science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) education has
  received great attention because it necessitates using various symbolic
  (e.g., text and formula) and graphical representations (e.g., pictures and
  graphs) to convey subject content. Research has mainly explored effects of
  combining two representations, but the potential benefits of integrating more
  than two representations on students' learning remain underexplored. This gap
  limits our understanding of promising educational practices and restricts the
  development of effective teaching strategies catering to students' cognitive
  needs. To close this gap, we conducted a systematic review of 46 studies and
  a meta-analysis that included 132 effect sizes to evaluate the effectiveness
  of using more than two representations in STEM education and to identify
  moderating factors influencing learning and problem-solving. A network
  diagram analysis revealed that the advantages of learning and problem-solving
  with MERs are also applicable to more than two representations. A subsequent
  meta-analysis revealed that the learning with more than two representations
  in STEM can have advantageous effects on students cognitive load
  ({\$}{\$}{\{}{\backslash}text{\{}Hedges{\}}{\}}{\{}^{\backslash}prime{\}}g
  =0.324,{\textasciitilde}p<.001,{\textasciitilde}95{\backslash}{\%}{\textasciitilde}{\backslash}text{\{}CI{\}}{\textasciitilde}[0.164,
  0.484]{\$}{\$}) and performance
  ({\$}{\$}{\{}{\backslash}text{\{}Hedges{\}}{\}}{\{}^{\backslash}prime{\}}g
  =0.118,{\textasciitilde}p<.001,{\textasciitilde}95{\backslash}{\%}{\textasciitilde}{\backslash}text{\{}CI{\}}{\textasciitilde}[0.050,
  0.185]{\$}{\$}) compared to learning with two representations without notable
  differences in learning time. The analysis of moderating factors revealed
  that benefits of learning with more than two representations primarily depend
  on the provision of appropriate support.%
    }
    \verb{doi}
    \verb 10.1007/s10648-024-09958-y
    \endverb
    \field{issn}{1573-336X}
    \field{number}{4}
    \field{pages}{124}
    \field{title}{The More the Better? A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of
  the Benefits of More than Two External Representations in STEM Education}
    \verb{url}
    \verb https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-024-09958-y
    \endverb
    \field{volume}{36}
    \field{journaltitle}{Educational Psychology Review}
    \field{year}{2024}
